{{nihongo|'''Kaneda'''|金田|Kaneda}}, officially the {{nihongo|'''Commonwealth Republic of Kaneda'''|金田共和国|Kaneda-Kyouwakoku}}, is a federal parliamentary republic located in southern [[Lurandia]] continent bordering [[Kazabune]] in south, [[United Midlands]] in east and (country A) in north. Covering an area of approximately 1.59 million square kilometres, the country has an estimated population of nearly 27 million people as of 2026. Its capital city is [[Nanaimo]], while [[Kanakou]] serves as its largest metropolitan and economic center. Kaneda is regarded as a highly developed nation, with a high-income economy, advanced infrastructure, and the highest human development ratings in Lurandia.

Kaneda traces its origins to a maritime settlement established in 1478 by the [[Kurokawa clan]], a powerful trading family from [[Yura Province]] in what is now [[Nakamizu]]. Seeking to dominate southern Lurandian trade routes, the clan founded a fortified port colony that gradually expanded through commerce, migration, and naval influence. During the late 16th century, the Kurokawa lost control of their mainland holdings in Ishiyama amid regional conflicts and political realignments. Large numbers of retainers, merchants, and settlers subsequently relocated to Kaneda, transforming the overseas settlement into the principal centre of Kurokawa authority.

Following the clan’s expulsion from Ishiyama in the 1590s, Kaneda effectively separated from mainland political structures and evolved into an autonomous successor state ruled by the exiled Kurokawa house. emerged from transplanted ruling elites displaced by upheaval in their homeland. Although the later [[Kawaei Shogunate]] restored Kurokawa influence in parts of mainland Nakamizu, Kaneda remained politically detached and developed an increasingly distinct identity.

The modern republican state was formally established on 4 March 1891 after the abolition of the old aristocratic order. Since the adoption of its current constitution in 1956, Kaneda has operated as a federal parliamentary republic with a bicameral legislature consisting of the Senate and the House of Commons. Its economy is heavily oriented toward maritime trade, shipping, advanced manufacturing, fisheries, and resource extraction, reflecting the country’s long-standing relationship with the sea. Often described as a “merchant republic of the southern ocean,” Kaneda maintains significant cultural and historical links with Nakamizu while preserving a distinct Kanedan national identity shaped by centuries of separation across the Shendan frontier.
== Etymology ==
The name '''Kaneda''' (金田) literally means “Golden Field” or “Golden Rice Field” in the Sanese language, derived from the characters ''kin/kane'' (金, “gold”) and ''ta/da'' (田, “field” or “farmland”). The term originally referred to the fertile river valley surrounding the first permanent Kurokawa settlement near present-day [[Nanaimo]] in southern [[Lurandia]].

According to early colonial chronicles of the [[Kurokawa clan]], the settlers were surprised by the exceptional agricultural productivity of the valley despite the region’s relatively high latitude and shorter growing season compared to mainland [[Nakamizu]]. Volcanic soils, abundant rainfall, and mild maritime conditions reportedly allowed unusually high crop yields for the area, particularly barley, millet, root vegetables, and later cold-resistant rice cultivars. The settlement consequently became known among traders and migrants as the “golden fields of Lurandia,” symbolizing both agricultural prosperity and the economic promise of the southern frontier.

The name gradually expanded from referring to the original settlement and valley into a broader geographic designation for Kurokawa-controlled territories in southern Lurandia. By the late 16th century, “Kaneda” had become the common political and cultural name for the emerging overseas state established by exiled Kurokawa retainers following their displacement from [[Yura Province]].

In modern usage, the official name of the state is '''Commonwealth Republic of Kaneda''' (金田共和国, ''Kaneda-Kyouwakoku''), though the shortened form “Kaneda” remains overwhelmingly more common in both domestic and international contexts.

